UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 2, Lesson 2: Why explorers travelled', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Explorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between empires and seeking new routes or resources. Motives are inferred from instructions, journals and later histories, and more than one motive can be true.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nGive two different motives that could send a ship to chart an unfamiliar coast, and name the kind of source that might show each.\n\nFEEDBACK\ne.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAssuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFor three published voyages, complete a motives table (trade / science / empire / other) with one source note each. Write one sentence on why motives can overlap.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Explorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between empires and seeking new routes or resources. Motives are inferred from instructions, journals and later histories, and more than one motive can be true.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: For three published voyages, complete a motives table (trade / science / empire / other) with one source note each. Write one sentence on why motives can overlap. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types. Reasoning: I used motive by attending to trade rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence. Correction: e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types. Why: the correction restores accurate use of motive.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating motive on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with motive, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types. This matches the lesson skill for motive.","options":["Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types.","Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence."],"question":"Give two different motives that could send a ship to chart an unfamiliar coast, and name the kind of source that might show each. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence.","Explorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between empires and seeking new routes or resources. Motives…","e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types.","Careful use of trade with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising motive?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named trade, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the trade used."],"question":"A learner is practising motive. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types.","Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about motive?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about motive.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about trade?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Example: a voyage instruction mentions mapping and reporting resources; a journal notes interest in plants; a later history links the…","Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Explorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["For three published voyages, complete a motives table (trade / science / empire / other) with one source note each. Write one sentence on why…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of motive?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating motive on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types. Reasoning: I used motive by attending to trade rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with motive, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence. Correction: e.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types. Why: the correction restores accurate use of motive.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y4-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise motive.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use motive with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify trade and explain why it matters.","I can practise science independently on a new prompt.","I can notice empire needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Explorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between empires and seeking new routes or resources. Motives are inferred from instructions, journals and later histories, and more than one motive can be true. Supported records…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Explorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between empires and seeking new routes or resources. Motives are inferred from instructions, journals and later histories, and more than one motive can be true.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about motive?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a voyage instruction mentions mapping and reporting resources; a journal notes interest in plants; a later history links the voyage to empire rivalry. The class lists motives separately and ticks which sources support each.\n\nTRY FIRST\nGive two different motives that could send a ship to chart an unfamiliar coast, and name the kind of source that might show each.\n\nFEEDBACK\ne.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types.","explain":"Explorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between empires and seeking new routes or resources. Motives are inferred from instructions, journals and later histories, and more than one motive can be true.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Why explorers travelled.","caption":"Mixed motives OK.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Motives</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">trade science empire</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Overlap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">more than one</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: a voyage instruction mentions mapping and reporting resources; a journal notes interest in plants; a later hist Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: a voyage instruction mentions mapping and reporting resources; a journal notes interest in plants; a later hist Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about motive?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a voyage instruction mentions mapping and reporting resources; a journal notes interest in plants; a later history links the voyage to empire rivalry. The class lists motives separately and ticks which sources support each. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Motives table.","caption":"Three voyages.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Evidence ticks</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">per motive</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">single motive only</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about motive?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAssuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence. Here is a more productive model: Example: a voyage instruction mentions mapping and reporting resources; a journal notes interest in plants; a later history links the voyage to empire rivalry. The class lists motives separately and ticks which sources support each.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\ne.g. trade/resources (company instructions); science/mapping (naturalist notes); empire rivalry (orders from a government) — any two with matching source types.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Assuming every explorer had only one simple motive ignores mixed evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about motive?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExplorers and traders travelled for mixed motives: trade goods, scientific curiosity, competition between empires and seeking new routes or resources. Motives are inferred from instructions, journals and later histories, and more than one motive can be true. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 2, Lesson 2: Why explorers travelled","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"First contacts and exploration","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a voyage instruction mentions mapping and reporting resources; a journal notes interest in plants; a later history links the voyage to empire rivalry. The class lists motives separately and ticks which sources support each.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Why explorers travelled.","caption":"Mixed motives OK.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Motives</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">trade science empire</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Overlap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">more than one</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Motives table.","caption":"Three voyages.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Evidence ticks</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">per motive</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">single motive only</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y4-w02-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
